  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday through next Friday)\nIssued at 1156 AM CST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\nMonday the broad scale high does start to flatten out into a more \nzonal flow.  Very mild conditions will continue with near normal \ntemperatures.  High pressure will build over the western part of the \nUS on Tuesday and will start to progress east.  As we move to \nWednesday and Thursday we start to get some divergence in the models \nand while it does appear that we'll have a weak frontal system \nWednesday afternoon that may bring down temperatures a few degrees, \nThursday is the bigger day in question.  For a few days now models \nhave, and continue to be split on an amplified high over the west to \ncentral CONUS with a deepening low over the Great Lakes region.  The \nsplit is how far west that colder air will expand, and that will \nhave a much larger impact on if we can expect highs in the 70s, to \npossibly 50s on Thursday.  Regardless of if we get the backdoor cold \nfront or it stays far enough east, Friday should be a transition day \nas the ridge builds further east and warmer air either holds or \nmoves over the Panhandles.  Right now NBM temperatures are very much \nseasonal, but the more probable highs on Thursday would be closer to \nthe 25th or 75th percentile and the same goes for Friday.  We'll \njust have to see how this cold airmass to the east plays out. \n\nWeber",
